INT. COURTROOM 4B - SENTENCING HEARING - DAY

Oak paneling, a flag that droops. A ceiling fan that has not turned since the 90s.

JUDGE HORTENSE ABARA (60s, reading glasses low on her nose, a face carved from patience) presides from the bench. Below her, defense attorney CORDELIA PASCOE (40s, black suit, red folder) stands at the table with her client's empty chair beside her.

                    ABARA
          Counsel, where is your client?

                    PASCOE
          Your Honor, she is on her way.

                    ABARA
          She was due at nine. It is nine-forty.

                    PASCOE
          She is stuck on the bridge.

                    ABARA
          Then I will wait.

The gallery rustles. The bailiff coughs. Pascoe opens her folder, closes it, opens it again.

                    PASCOE
          Your Honor, may I approach?

                    ABARA
          You may not.

                    PASCOE
          It concerns the motion.

                    ABARA
          Denied.

                    PASCOE
          I haven't made it.

                    ABARA
          I know what is in it.

Pascoe sets her pen down. Abara removes her glasses, folds them, sets them on the bench. She looks at Pascoe as one looks at a long-standing wound.

                    PASCOE
          You've decided already.

                    ABARA
          I decided in March.

                    PASCOE
          Then why make her come?

                    ABARA
          Because she should hear it with her own ears.

Pascoe crosses to the empty chair. Rests her hand on its back. Her knuckles whiten. Abara watches her do it, picks up her gavel, puts it down without striking, and leans forward with both elbows on the bench while Pascoe turns her whole body toward the gallery, scans each face, and then stares straight back up at the bench, jaw working, one foot already moving toward the aisle.

                    PASCOE
          She's not coming.

                    ABARA
          No.

                    PASCOE
          You knew.

                    ABARA
          She called my chambers at eight.

                    PASCOE
          And you let me stand here.

                    ABARA
          I wanted to see what you would do.

Pascoe laughs once, a dry, helpless sound. She picks up her folder and hurls it at the table. Papers fly. The bailiff starts forward. Abara raises a hand and he stops.

                    PASCOE
          Do you know what she told me about you?

                    ABARA
          Tell me.

                    PASCOE
          That you were the only one who ever looked at her.

Abara says nothing. The fan overhead gives one slow, grinding turn.
